<h2>The transaction: jewellery on credit, a frozen account, a bounced expectation</h2>
<p>The second respondent, a jewellery concern, alleged that it supplied gold jewellery worth approximately ₹1.48 crore on credit, induced by assurances from the accused side about short-term liquidity and repayment. Payment did not come. A cheque of ₹60 lakh entered the story, as did a bank account marked "debit frozen". An FIR followed at P.S. Tilak Nagar under Sections 318 and 61 BNS. The petitioner — whose mother, a co-accused, and he had earlier obtained bail — moved the High Court under Article 226 read with Section 528 BNSS to quash the FIR itself.</p>

<h2>The rival framings</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h4>The petitioner\'s case</h4><p>A commercial dispute dressed as crime: a running trade relationship, prompt earlier payments reflected in the FIR itself, no dishonest intention at inception — at worst a recovery claim for a civil court.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h4>The Court\'s reading</h4><p>The FIR alleges inducement, not just default: assurances of liquidity and repayment that persuaded the supplier to part with stock on credit. Whether the intent behind those assurances was dishonest from the start requires evidence — precisely what a quashing court cannot weigh.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The reasoning in brief</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Breach alone is not cheating</h4><p>The Court accepted the settled proposition that a mere breach of commercial obligation, without dishonest intent at inception, does not constitute cheating.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Past payments are not an alibi</h4><p>Prompt earlier payments were relevant but could not conclusively negate dishonest intention for the later, larger supplies — a pattern of small performances can itself build the confidence that a larger deception exploits.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Threshold test only</h4><p>Applying <em>Neeharika Infrastructure</em>, the Court confined itself to whether the FIR disclosed a cognizable offence. The cheque, the frozen account and the conspiracy allegations all needed investigation, not threshold adjudication.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The petition was dismissed, with the standard protections: the observations bind no one at trial, the investigation proceeds in accordance with law, and all contentions remain open.</p>

<h2>What separates civil default from criminal cheating</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Civil recovery</th><th>Cheating (S.318 BNS)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Core element</td><td>Breach of a promise to pay or perform</td><td>Deception inducing delivery of property, with dishonest intent at inception</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Timing of intent</td><td>Irrelevant — liability follows breach</td><td>Decisive — intent must exist when the inducement was made</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Forum</td><td>Civil or commercial court; summary suit where available</td><td>Police investigation and criminal trial</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Typical evidence</td><td>Invoices, ledgers, acknowledgments</td><td>The assurances given, the parties\' conduct before and after supply, financial capacity at the time</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practical lessons</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For suppliers extending credit:</strong> record the assurances on which credit is extended — messages, emails and meeting notes often decide whether an FIR survives the quashing test.</li>
    <li><strong>For accused persons:</strong> a quashing petition built only on the "commercial dispute" label is unlikely to succeed where the FIR pleads inducement; the stronger course may be to contest the case on evidence while pursuing settlement where appropriate.</li>
    <li><strong>For both sides:</strong> parallel civil recovery and criminal process can lawfully coexist; neither forecloses the other, though settlements routinely resolve both together.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>The judgment protects the boundary from both directions: creditors cannot criminalise every default, but debtors cannot decriminalise every deception by calling it business.</p>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. Whether particular facts disclose cheating is a question requiring case-specific assessment.</p></div>
